thin that specific locker.\n\n#### 2. Open Addressing\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map searches for the next \"empty\" slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, etc., until an empty spot is found.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** Instead of checking linearly,"}]}
{"benchmark": "diffusion", "args": {"family": "diffusion", "model": "google/diffusiongemma-26B-A4B-it", "device": "auto", "dtype": "bfloat16", "compile": "auto", "expert_parallel": true, "experts_implementation": "by_token_count", "batch_sizes": [16, 2, 8], "variant_batch_size": 1, "variants": ["grouped"], "prompts": "chat", "prompt_length": 64, "prompt_bucket": 64, "max_new_tokens": 512, "stop_check_interval": 4, "warmup_runs": 2, "timed_runs": 3, "gsm8k_examples": 0, "gsm8k_batch_size": 10, "seed": 0, "label": "diffusion_b16", "output": "/opt/dgemma/repo/results/session2/results.jsonl", "batch_size": 16, "sampler": "static", "cache": "static", "max_denoising_steps": null, "attn_implementation": null, "do_sample": false}, "environment": {"torch": "2.15.0+cpu", "transformers": "5.22.2", "python": "3.12.16", "device": "tpu", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T12:10:38Z", "torch_tpu": "unknown", "device_count": 1}, "load_seconds": 14.375604231999887, "warmup_seconds": [270.056651614, 21.591185816999996], "median_tokens_per_second": 329.0681320430691, "median_canvas_tokens_per_second": 344.72201249319977, "median_total_seconds": 23.828563127000052, "peak_memory_bytes": 30405239296, "runs": [{"num_denoising_steps": 84, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 20, "decoder_forward_passes": [24, 39, 35, 47, 26, 35, 27, 20, 26, 48, 55, 29, 34, 71, 26, 11], "tokens_per_forward": [21.33333396911621, 11.307692527770996, 14.628572463989258, 10.893617630004883, 19.69230842590332, 14.628572463989258, 18.962963104248047, 25.600000381469727, 19.69230842590332, 10.666666984558105, 9.309091567993164, 17.65517234802246, 15.05882453918457, 7.211267948150635, 19.69230842590332, 19.18181800842285], "prefill_seconds": 0.06448742800012042, "canvas_seconds": [13.677011810000067, 10.08475453899996], "total_seconds": 23.828563127000052, "generated_tokens": 7820, "canvas_tokens": 8192, "tokens_per_second": 329.0681320430691, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 344.72201249319977, "seconds_per_step": 0.28290566308333254, "token_checksum": 446815166, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, we first have to understand why they happen. \n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string or integer) into an index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is often infinite (or massive) while the array is finite, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of the primary strategies used to handle collisions and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Hashing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of each slot in the array storing a single value, each slot stores a pointer to a **linked list** (or another data structure like a tree).\n\n* **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new entry is simply appended to the list located at index 5.\n* **Lookup process:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find the index, then traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n* **Pros:** Simple to implement; handles overflow gracefully.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map \"probes\" for the next available empty slot. There are three main probing strategies:\n\n* **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n* **Quadratic Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1^2$, then $i+2^2$, etc., to avoid \"clustering\" (clumps of filled slots).\n* **"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 48, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 12, "decoder_forward_passes": [26, 40, 41, 28, 28, 24, 34, 15, 27, 38, 43, 21, 31, 27, 25, 27], "tokens_per_forward": [19.69230842590332, 12.800000190734863, 12.487805366516113, 18.285715103149414, 18.285715103149414, 21.33333396911621, 15.05882453918457, 15.066667556762695, 18.962963104248047, 10.947368621826172, 11.906976699829102, 24.380952835083008, 16.516130447387695, 18.962963104248047, 20.48000144958496, 18.962963104248047], "prefill_seconds": 0.06426161800004593, "canvas_seconds": [6.954003852999904, 6.720464071999913], "total_seconds": 13.74163799300004, "generated_tokens": 7810, "canvas_tokens": 8192, "tokens_per_second": 571.0159453004014, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 598.9452783483853, "seconds_per_step": 0.2849453411458332, "token_checksum": 432853802, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually map to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of how hash maps resolve these conflicts and how it affects performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Resolution Strategy A: Separate Chaining (Linked Lists)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of each slot in the array holding a single value, each slot (or \"bucket\") points to another data structure\u2014usually a **Linked List** or a balanced tree.\n\n*   **How it works:** When two keys hash to the same index, they are simply appended to the list at that index. \n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find the bucket, then traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n\n### 2. Resolution Strategy B: Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this strategy, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available empty slot.\n\nThere are three common types of \"probing\":\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1^2$, then $i+2^2$, etc. (to avoid \"clustering\"). \n*   **Double Hashing:** Use a second hash function t"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 8