{"benchmark": "diffusion", "args": {"family": "diffusion", "model": "google/diffusiongemma-26B-A4B-it", "device": "auto", "dtype": "bfloat16", "compile": "auto", "expert_parallel": true, "experts_implementation": "by_token_count", "batch_sizes": [16, 2, 8], "variant_batch_size": 1, "variants": ["grouped"], "prompts": "chat", "prompt_length": 64, "prompt_bucket": 64, "max_new_tokens": 512, "stop_check_interval": 4, "warmup_runs": 2, "timed_runs": 3, "gsm8k_examples": 0, "gsm8k_batch_size": 10, "seed": 0, "label": "diffusion_b2", "output": "/opt/dgemma/repo/results/session2/results.jsonl", "batch_size": 2, "sampler": "static", "cache": "static", "max_denoising_steps": null, "attn_implementation": null, "do_sample": false}, "environment": {"torch": "2.15.0+cpu", "transformers": "5.22.2", "python": "3.12.16", "device": "tpu", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T12:12:27Z", "torch_tpu": "unknown", "device_count": 1}, "load_seconds": 16.28772921399991, "warmup_seconds": [92.60137465699995, 1.6571636099999978], "median_tokens_per_second": 731.4382206661393, "median_canvas_tokens_per_second": 731.7703803278627, "median_total_seconds": 1.4470180209998489, "peak_memory_bytes": 30405239296, "runs": [{"num_denoising_steps": 36, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 9, "decoder_forward_passes": [24, 33], "tokens_per_forward": [21.33333396911621, 12.030303001403809], "prefill_seconds": 0.020458250000046974, "canvas_seconds": [0.5962371160001112, 0.4557763769998928], "total_seconds": 1.0748003730000164, "generated_tokens": 909, "canvas_tokens": 1024, "tokens_per_second": 862.1489933586067, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 971.2217482939641, "seconds_per_step": 0.029287281194443595, "token_checksum": 44391497, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, we first have to understand the ideal scenario: a hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer index in an underlying array (often called a \"bucket\").\n\nA **collision** occurs when two different keys are mapped to the same index by the hash function. Since the number of possible keys is usually infinite and the array size is finite, collisions are mathematically inevitable.\n\nHere are the two primary ways hash maps handle this problem:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing the data directly in the array slot, each bucket slot acts as a pointer to another data structure (usually a **Linked List** or a balanced tree).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** The map hashes the key, goes to the index, and then traverses the linked list until it finds the matching key.\n*   **Modern variation:** In Java 8+, if a bucket (list) grows too long (e.g., more than 8 items), it converts the linked list into a **Red-Black Tree** to speed up searches.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly in the array. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available empty slot.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, it checks $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** It use"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 48, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 12, "decoder_forward_passes": [29, 44], "tokens_per_forward": [17.65517234802246, 11.636363983154297], "prefill_seconds": 0.04703648999998222, "canvas_seconds": [0.5977095460000328, 0.7992867549999119], "total_seconds": 1.4470180209998489, "generated_tokens": 1024, "canvas_tokens": 1024, "tokens_per_second": 731.4382206661393, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 731.4382206661393, "seconds_per_step": 0.029166281895830554, "token_checksum": 49292850, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand what a collision is.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps manage this situation and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Common Collision-Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Linked Lists)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of each slot in the array (called a \"bucket\") holding a single value, it holds a pointer to a secondary data structure, usually a linked list or a balanced search tree.\n*   **How it works:** When two keys hash to the same index, the new entry is simply appended to the linked list at that index.\n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, the map hashes the key, finds the bucket, and then traverses the linked list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available empty slot. There are three main types of probing:\n*   **Linear Probing:** Check the next index ($i+1, i+2, \\dots$).\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** Check indices using a quadratic formula ($i+1^2, i+2^2, \\dots$).\n*   **Double Hashing:** Use a second hash function to determine the \"ste"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 48, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 12, "decoder_forward_passes": [22, 45], "tokens_per_forward": [23.272727966308594, 10.199999809265137], "prefill_seconds": 0.09864446900019175, "canvas_seconds": [0.711511986000005, 0.684763576000023], "total_seconds": 1.4979905310001413, "generated_tokens": 971, "canvas_tokens": 1024, "tokens_per_second": 693.8955461897995, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 731.7703803278627, "seconds_per_step": 0.029153042958332282, "token_checksum": 48221500, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, we first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key into an integer index in an array. Because the number of possible keys is effectively infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of how hash maps resolve these conflicts and how it affects performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Common Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Open Lists)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing a single value directly in the array slot, each slot (bucket) points to a secondary data structure\u2014usually a linked list or a balanced tree.\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup:** To find a value, the map goes to the index and traverses the list until it finds the correct key.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Closed Addressing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array. If a collision occurs, the map \"probes\" for the next available empty slot.\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, check $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on.\n*   **Quadratic Probing:** Uses a quadratic formula to find the next slot (e.g., $i+1^2, i+2^2$) to avoid \"clustering.\"\n*   **Double Hashing:** Uses a second hash function to determine the \"step size\" for finding the next slot.\n\n-"}]}
